{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during/after regime shifts downward. Apply a downward level-shift correction when recent realized trend is negative.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when the last 1–2 weeks show a clear selloff, the model continues to project sideways-to-higher paths. Add a momentum filter: if 10–15d slope is negative, force the forecast path to drift down for the first ~10–20 days.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following a spike/high plateau in history, the model extrapolates elevated levels and even re-accelerates upward, while outcomes mean-revert lower. Cap upside and enforce mean reversion toward a rolling 20–30d mean when history is near the top of its 30d range.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: bias grows with horizon (later days are most overstated). Use a horizon ramp: small correction days 1–5, larger correction days 10–30 (increasing downward adjustment with lead time).",
    "Volatility/shape mismatch: model produces frequent upward bumps that do not occur in outcomes during selloffs. Smooth the forecast path under bearish regimes (reduce day-to-day oscillations and remove late-horizon rebound humps)."
  ]
}
